\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Full $5 \times 5$ threshold-transfer matrix. Each row gives the normalized recovery ratio of the $\tau $ tuned on the row dataset, evaluated on each of the five column datasets. Positive values are not always ``better'' when the baseline$-$v$1$ denominator is negative. Rows can be byte-identical across the eval columns when two tune datasets pick the same $\tau ^\ast $: tune-SQuAD and tune-HotpotQA both pick $\tau ^\ast = 0.3$, and tune-PubMedQA and tune-TriviaQA both pick $\tau ^\ast = 0.4$, so the off-diagonal columns coincide. This is a faithful consequence of the experiment (off-diagonal recovery only depends on the eval dataset and the selected $\tau $, not on which tune dataset selected it), not a pipeline bug.}}{9}{table.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:supp:threshold_transfer_full}{{14}{9}{Full $5 \times 5$ threshold-transfer matrix. Each row gives the normalized recovery ratio of the $\tau $ tuned on the row dataset, evaluated on each of the five column datasets. Positive values are not always ``better'' when the baseline$-$v$1$ denominator is negative. Rows can be byte-identical across the eval columns when two tune datasets pick the same $\tau ^\ast $: tune-SQuAD and tune-HotpotQA both pick $\tau ^\ast = 0.3$, and tune-PubMedQA and tune-TriviaQA both pick $\tau ^\ast = 0.4$, so the off-diagonal columns coincide. This is a faithful consequence of the experiment (off-diagonal recovery only depends on the eval dataset and the selected $\tau $, not on which tune dataset selected it), not a pipeline bug}{table.14}{}}
